extras={
'ch14-import-OBJ_2131525':('Ovulation',[S(10,2,1),'L10-W2'],'Ovulation is located at the ovary, and worked step 2 explicitly says oviduct blockage does not itself remove this event. Unknown distractor terminology is not needed to choose the supported event.','Recognition/application'),
'ch14-import-OBJ_2131570':('Estrogen and progesterone',[S(9,4,1)],'The paragraph says combined methods supply an estrogen and a progestin and defines progestin as progesterone-like. This supports the option phrased as hormones similar to estrogen and progesterone.','Recognition'),
'ch14-import-OBJ_2131114':('Menopause',[S(9,2,1)],'The paragraph defines menopause in the terms used by the prompt.','Retrieval'),
'ch14-sequence-08':('1. Developing trophoblast tissue releases hCG → 2. hCG supports the corpus luteum → 3. The corpus luteum continues hormone release → 4. Progesterone helps maintain the endometrium',[S(9,3,1),S(9,3,2),S(9,3,3)],'03-p3 states the complete causal order. The supplied steps are already stored/displayed in this order; this answer verifies a readable sequence only, not independent ordering or a native shuffle.','Pathway reconstruction; display order cues answer'),
'ch14-application-16':('Less ovarian hormone feedback allows greater pituitary stimulation',[S(9,2,2),S(9,2,3)],'Reduced ovarian output removes inhibition; the functioning anterior pituitary can release more FSH/LH even while the target responds poorly.','Close application/recognition'),
'ch14-application-16-flash':('If follicles respond less strongly, their ovarian hormone output can fall. With less inhibitory feedback, a functioning hypothalamic–pituitary system allows the anterior pituitary to release more FSH and LH. More gonadotropin stimulation does not guarantee that poorly responsive follicles recover.',[S(9,2,2),S(9,2,3),'L09-W2'],'The paragraphs and worked step 2 supply the entire weak response → less inhibition → higher pituitary output chain.','Taught scenario explanation'),
'ch14-application-17':('Reduced likelihood of follicle maturation and ovulation',[S(9,4,1),'L09-W3'],'04-p1 links reduced FSH/LH support to reduced follicular development and suppressed LH surge to reduced ovulation; worked step 3 connects ovarian responsiveness to needed stimulation.','Close application/recognition'),
'ch14-application-17-flash':('The intended consequence is less follicle maturation and a lower likelihood of ovulation. The ovaries receive reduced FSH/LH stimulation, and suppressing the LH surge removes the normal signal needed for ovulation.',[S(9,4,1),'L09-W3'],'The same taught causal links explain the intended ovarian consequence without requiring unseen information.','Taught scenario explanation'),
'ch14-application-18':('Absence of symptoms alone cannot rule out an infection',[S(10,1,3)],'Asymptomatic infection and the limits of appearance are explicitly stated.','Direct recognition'),
'ch14-application-18-flash':('No visible symptoms do not establish that there is no STI. Some infections are asymptomatic, so appearance or lack of discomfort alone cannot rule infection out.',[S(10,1,3)],'The paragraph directly defines asymptomatic and explains the inference limit.','Explain stated evidence limitation'),
'ch14-application-19':('The route needed for gametes to meet is disrupted',[S(10,2,1),S(10,2,2)],'Follicular/ovarian events are separated from oviduct transport, which scar blockage can disrupt.','Close application/recognition'),
'ch14-application-19-flash':('Follicles may still develop at the ovary, but scar tissue can obstruct the oviduct route through which sperm and the oocyte would meet. A functioning source of gametes does not repair the transport pathway, so fertility can be reduced.',[S(10,2,1),S(10,2,2),S(10,2,3)],'The teaching locates the separate functions and states transport impairment can contribute to infertility. One affected duct is phrased as a reduction in fertility, not a guarantee that all reproduction stops.','Taught scenario explanation'),
'ch14-application-20':('It can reduce transmission risk but does not eliminate every risk',[S(10,4,2)],'The paragraph explains barrier action and continuing transmission risk from affected uncovered skin.','Direct recognition'),
'ch14-application-20-flash':('An appropriate condom used correctly and consistently acts as a physical barrier that reduces contact with infectious material. It can lower transmission risk, but affected skin outside the covered area can still transmit some infections, so the protection is not absolute.',[S(10,4,2)],'The paragraph supplies both the positive mechanism and its limitation.','Reconstruct stated mechanism'),
'ch14-typed-19':('hCG',[S(9,3,2)],'The early-pregnancy signal supporting the corpus luteum is explicitly named.','Retrieval'),
'ch14-typed-20':('Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)',[S(10,2,2)],'The paragraph names the condition and abbreviation after describing spread into upper reproductive structures.','Scenario retrieval'),
'ch14-multiple-select-5':('a and b: Some infections may be present without obvious symptoms; scarring in an oviduct can interfere with reproduction.',[S(10,1,3),S(10,2,2),S(10,2,3)],'01-p3 supports a, rejects diagnosis from an online symptom list, and names both bacterial and viral infections (rejecting c and d). 02-p2/3 supports b.','Discriminate multiple stated conclusions')}
for i,g in enumerate(gen,1):
    gid=g['id'];page=1 if i<=10 else 2 if i<=23 else 3 if i<=33 else 4 if i<=38 else 5 if i<=44 else 6
    if gid.startswith('ch14-concept-'):
        tail=gid[len('ch14-concept-'):];key,variant=tail.rsplit('-',1);l,explain,term,mis,refs,mrefs=concepts[key]
